 "headline": [
  {
   "k": "portfolio-v1",
   "v": "80%",
   "s": "random kingdoms, every card playable"
  },
  {
   "k": "Steam cards",
   "v": "26 / 26",
   "s": "all base-set cards playable by the autopilot"
  }
 ],
 "entries": [
  {
   "when": "14 November 2026, evening",
   "title": "Plan: a portfolio of specialists, picked per kingdom",
   "html": "<p>Steam deals 10 of the 26 cards at random, and a GA genome trained on one fixed kingdom only knows those 10. The new model is a <b>portfolio</b>: about twenty specialists, each evolved with <b>all 26 cards</b> on fresh random kingdoms that always hold its key cards (Witch, Militia, Smithy, Laboratory, Library, Gardens + Workshop, Village + Smithy\u2026), plus a generalist for any kingdom.</p><p>The picker is a model too, but not a learned one: when a game starts it plays every specialist on <i>that exact kingdom</i> against the benchmark bots on the C engine (about 25,000 games per second) and the best one plays the game. The new trainer is <code>ga/specialist.py</code>, the model kind <code>ga-portfolio</code> (<code>policy/portfolio.py</code>), and the model goes in its own folder, <code>models/portfolio-v1/</code>.</p><p>The nine cards the autopilot can't play on Steam yet (Cellar, Remodel, Library\u2026) are in training. During play they are never bought, and the picker tries each specialist the same way, so it picks one that wins without them. One run, <i>generalist-safe</i>, trains without them as a fallback.</p>"
  },
  {
   "when": "14 November 2026, 19:50",
   "title": "A benchmark bug, fixed: every kingdom now gets its own games",
   "html": "<p>The first Witch specialist stalled at the same validation score many times, and the per-bot numbers were suspiciously round (95.0%, 62.5%). The cause: every kingdom in a test set reused the same game seeds. When neither player buys the other kingdom cards, a Witch-and-money deck plays <i>exactly the same game</i> on all 60 kingdoms, so \u201c60 kingdoms \u00d7 10 seeds\u201d was really 10 games per seat. The model benchmark (<code>policy.bench</code>) had the same flaw.</p><p>Fixed in both: each kingdom gets its own seeds (benchmark version 3), and all models were re-measured. The best old model, k4-20260614, scores <b>78.9%</b> (it was 81.5%), and k1-steam, the model behind every Steam game so far, scores <b>43.7%</b>. Those are the numbers to beat. Training restarted with the fix: 21 specialists \u00d7 500 generations, about two hours.</p>"
  },
  {
   "when": "14 November 2026, 19:57",
   "title": "First two specialists: generalist 71%, Witch 84%",
   "html": "<p>The <b>generalist</b> (any kingdom, all 26 cards) climbed from 59% to <b>71.2%</b> in 500 generations (5 minutes): 79% against Big Money, 71% against Smithy\u2013Big Money, 63% against Witch\u2013Big Money. For comparison, k1-steam scores 43.7% on random kingdoms.</p><p>The <b>Witch</b> specialist reached <b>83.8%</b> on kingdoms with a Witch (97% / 93% / 60% against the three bots). The best old model, k4-20260614, scores 78.9% on its own Witch kingdoms. Nineteen specialists to go.</p>"
  },
  {
   "when": "14 November 2026, 21:45",
   "title": "portfolio-v1 released: 79% on random kingdoms, almost twice k1-steam",
   "html": "<p>All 21 specialists finished (500 generations each, about 5 minutes per specialist). They became one model, <code>models/portfolio-v1/</code>, together with the best old model k4-20260614. It is now the default and the selector's first choice.</p>\n<p><b>Test:</b> 200 random Steam kingdoms the model never saw, 40 games per seat against each fitting bot, played the way the autopilot plays today (the nine blocked cards are never bought):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"t\"><tr><th>Player</th><th class=\"num\">Score</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>portfolio-v1</b> (picks per kingdom)</td><td class=\"num\"><b>79.3%</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>best possible pick in hindsight</td><td class=\"num\">81.9%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>best single specialist (remodel, never buying Remodel)</td><td class=\"num\">75.7%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>k4-20260614 (old best)</td><td class=\"num\">50.9%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>k1-steam (every Steam game so far)</td><td class=\"num\">41.9%</td></tr></table></div>\n<p>The pick works: it gets within about two and a half points of a perfect choice and beats every single specialist. With all 26 cards allowed the portfolio would score 80.1%, so teaching the autopilot the nine missing cards is worth about one and a half points overall. The Gardens + Workshop specialist is the clearest case: 87.6% in training but 6.9% without Workshop, so it is never picked today. Workshop is the first card to teach the autopilot.</p>\n<p>The stored benchmark (<code>policy.bench</code>) is <b>75.0%</b>: 87% against Big Money, 74% against Smithy\u2013Big Money, 64% against Witch\u2013Big Money.</p>\n<details><summary>Every member alone (200 kingdoms) and how often it was picked</summary><div class=\"tablewrap\"><table class=\"t\"><tr><th>Member</th><th class=\"num\">Steam (blocked cards off)</th><th class=\"num\">all cards</th><th class=\"num\">picked</th></tr><tr><td>remodel</td><td class='num'>75.7%</td><td class='num'>75.7%</td><td class='num'>14</td></tr><tr><td>generalist-safe</td><td class='num'>73.8%</td><td class='num'>73.8%</td><td class='num'>35</td></tr><tr><td>generalist</td><td class='num'>73.5%</td><td class='num'>73.3%</td><td class='num'>24</td></tr><tr><td>sentry</td><td class='num'>72.9%</td><td class='num'>72.9%</td><td class='num'>9</td></tr><tr><td>market</td><td class='num'>71.6%</td><td class='num'>73.1%</td><td class='num'>20</td></tr><tr><td>smithy</td><td class='num'>71.1%</td><td class='num'>71.1%</td><td class='num'>12</td></tr><tr><td>village-smithy</td><td class='num'>71.0%</td><td class='num'>71.0%</td><td class='num'>14</td></tr><tr><td>artisan</td><td class='num'>70.6%</td><td class='num'>71.6%</td><td class='num'>7</td></tr><tr><td>council-room</td><td class='num'>70.6%</td><td class='num'>70.7%</td><td class='num'>21</td></tr><tr><td>militia</td><td class='num'>70.0%</td><td class='num'>69.9%</td><td class='num'>8</td></tr><tr><td>bandit</td><td class='num'>69.9%</td><td class='num'>69.9%</td><td class='num'>4</td></tr><tr><td>chapel</td><td class='num'>69.6%</td><td class='num'>69.6%</td><td class='num'>4</td></tr><tr><td>bureaucrat</td><td class='num'>68.0%</td><td class='num'>68.0%</td><td class='num'>1</td></tr><tr><td>festival</td><td class='num'>67.6%</td><td class='num'>67.6%</td><td class='num'>6</td></tr><tr><td>mine</td><td class='num'>67.6%</td><td class='num'>69.0%</td><td class='num'>6</td></tr><tr><td>throne-room</td><td class='num'>65.5%</td><td class='num'>67.3%</td><td class='num'>0</td></tr><tr><td>laboratory</td><td class='num'>65.4%</td><td class='num'>65.4%</td><td class='num'>2</td></tr><tr><td>library</td><td class='num'>62.3%</td><td class='num'>65.6%</td><td class='num'>5</td></tr><tr><td>moneylender</td><td class='num'>62.2%</td><td class='num'>62.2%</td><td class='num'>2</td></tr><tr><td>witch</td><td class='num'>61.9%</td><td class='num'>61.9%</td><td class='num'>4</td></tr><tr><td>k4-20260614</td><td class='num'>50.9%</td><td class='num'>50.9%</td><td class='num'>2</td></tr><tr><td>gardens</td><td class='num'>6.9%</td><td class='num'>17.5%</td><td class='num'>0</td></tr></table></div></details>\n<p><b>Not tested yet:</b> real Steam games. The portfolio buys cards k1-steam never bought (Bandit, Bureaucrat, Council Room, Festival, Gardens\u2026), so the first games should be watched.</p>"
  },
  {
   "when": "15 November 2026, 01:45",
   "title": "Every card is playable on Steam now",
   "html": "<p>The nine blocked cards are open. Two halves:</p>\n<p><b>Protocol 2</b> (the model side): every card's own choices have a request (Cellar, Poacher, Remodel, Workshop, Artisan, Mine, Moneylender, Harbinger, Vassal, Library, Sentry, and Bandit / Bureaucrat when attacked). Each answer is the engine's own decision for that card, asked of the same GA agent that played in training. A test plays 300 engine games and checks, at every card choice, that the answer a player gets equals what the genome did: identical.</p>\n<p><b>The autopilot</b> learned each prompt on the real game, in four games against Very Easy on kingdoms made of the new cards, with a test model that buys them: gains are clicks on the glowing supply pile (Workshop, Remodel, Artisan, Mine), Cellar and Poacher are a hand selection plus OK or <i>Decline</i>, Harbinger and Vassal pick from a pop-up, Library has a <i>Set Aside</i> button, and <b>Sentry needs drag and drop</b> into Trash / Discard / Deck zones. Throne Room had no handler at all before; it does now (Throne Room \u2192 Artisan twice works).</p>\n<p>Bugs found on the way, all fixed: the \u201cMilitia (0 of 2)\u201d counter was read as \u201cdiscard 0\u201d; a hover tooltip was taken for a discard prompt; the coin count ignored Moneylender, Vassal and Poacher; an unreadable coin counter made a successful Province buy look failed; an empty Estate pile wasn't seen, so Workshop clicked it 58 times; and the GA agent itself kept a Province over a Gold when attacked by Militia (fixed in the Python and C agents).</p>\n<p><b>First real game with all cards:</b> portfolio-v1 against Easy on Gardens, Workshop, Cellar, Remodel, Library, Sentry, Artisan, Harbinger, Vassal, Poacher. The portfolio picked its <b>Gardens + Workshop</b> specialist (98% in its trial on that kingdom, unusable before) and won <b>34\u201333</b>. With every card allowed portfolio-v1 scores <b>80.3%</b> on random kingdoms (79.3% before).</p>\n<p>Not yet seen on Steam: the Moat reveal and the Bandit victim's choice (handlers exist). Overnight: round 2 of training, each specialist against the bots <i>and</i> three strong round-1 specialists.</p>"
  }
 ]
